  "verbalization": "Acrivastine is shown to interact with Dehydrogenase/reductase SDR family member 9, which is a target of multiple compounds, including N-Methyltyramine and a complex epoxy compound. All these targets ultimately lead to a relationship with HepG2, a human liver cancer cell line. The evidence suggests that Acrivastine may inhibit metabolic pathways in HepG2 cells through the inhibition of SDR family members, which are involved in the metabolism of various xenobiotics and endogenous compounds. The final scores indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, supported by the presence of multiple distinct targets that are all connected to HepG2. The mechanism appears to involve the modulation of metabolic processes, potentially through the inhibition of enzymes that are critical for cellular function in HepG2 cells. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Acrivastine to exert a biological effect on HepG2 cells via the SDR family, though further experimental validation is required to confirm the specific mechanism and therapeutic relevance.",
